► **ANSWER**

## Analysis

**What was failing and why:**
The build failed with `E0004: non-exhaustive patterns: &Component::Prefix(_)` not covered. Rust's exhaustiveness checker requires all variants of `Component` to be handled in a match, even if a variant is unreachable on a given platform. `Component::Prefix` is a Windows-only variant, but it still exists in the enum on Linux and must be matched.

Because the build failed, the binary `build/rust_mkdir` was never produced, causing the checkpoint tests to fail with "No such file or directory."

## Fix

Add a match arm for `Component::Prefix(_)` in the `mkdir_p` function's loop. Since this variant is unreachable on Linux, treat it as a no-op `continue`.

## Edit

In the `for (i, comp) in components.iter().enumerate()` loop, the match on `comp` currently has four arms. Add a fifth arm after `Component::RootDir`:

```rust
            Component::Prefix(_) => continue,
```

So the full match becomes:

```rust
        match comp {
            Component::CurDir => continue,
            Component::ParentDir => {
                current.push("..");
                continue;
            }
            Component::RootDir => {
                current = OsString::from("/");
                continue;
            }
            Component::Prefix(_) => continue,
            Component::Normal(name) => {
                current.push(name);
            }
        }
```

This is the only change required. It satisfies the compiler's exhaustiveness check, produces no behavioral change on Linux (the arm is unreachable), and will allow the binary to build successfully so the checkpoint tests can execute.
